Subsets of size 2: {1,3}, {1,5}, {1,7}, {3,5}, {3,7}, {5,7} – that's 6.

Subsets of size 3: {1,3,5}, {1,3,7}, {1,5,7}, {3,5,7} – 4.

Subsets of size 4: {1,3,5,7} – 1.

Adding those up: 4 + 6 is 10, plus 4 is 14, plus 1 is 15. Yep, that matches the earlier result. So that gives me more confidence that 15 is the right answer. 

Wait, but let me just think again if there's any chance I could have made a mistake here. The original set had eight elements, four of which are odd. Since subsets are formed independently, the number of subsets with only the odd numbers is indeed based only on those four. So all the non-empty ones would be as calculated. 

